title: 'Audit P2 closes the P0 list: Warpline moves from silently wrong to loudly honest'
summary: |
  Closed C-6 (truncation was undetectable — the witness was already on disk and unread), C-13 (a repair path where hand-editing JSONL was the documented recovery), C-14 (the MCP bearer token rode in every backup), and C-16 (shadow refusals audited clean). 852 passing, zero skipped. That completes all eight P0 items from the soundness audit. Re-scoring the audit's own grid: durability, recoverability, immutable history and the repair path all moved from ABSENT/PARTIAL to HOLDS. The honest verdict shifts from "not ready to be the only copy of anything" to "sound enough for a single writer, not yet for a swarm" — because the concurrency surface still has never executed in anger.</summary>
  <body>## The shape of what was fixed

  Every P0 defect shared one shape: the tool reported success while something was lost or exposed. A merge that sealed CLEAN on the wrong bytes. A verify that said "all intact" over a laundered lie, or over a fabric cut from 64 strands to 26. A backup that promised "secrets never travel" while carrying a bearer token with no expiry and no revocation. A shadow refusal returning isError:false.

  That is why the count matters less than the character change. A source-control tool's core promise is not "never fails" — it is "never lies about failing." The system moved from silently wrong to loudly honest.

  ## Three judgment calls the builders made that I did not specify

  **Repair handles TAIL tears only; mid-ledger corruption REFUSES.** My brief said "truncate to the last well-formed strand." Applied to mid-ledger damage that discards every good strand after it — repair becomes the data-loss event. The builder specialized it and said so explicitly rather than following the letter.

  **C-6 is advisory-in, hard-out.** An absent, unreadable, disabled or fresh journal yields an empty attestation set and cannot fail. A check that fired on every non-staking repo would teach people to ignore verify, which is worse than the hole. Severity was argued from symmetry rather than taste: ref-unresolved is ALREADY hard for the same class of evidence, and a stake has stronger provenance than a local ref because it is a git commit that can be pushed off-machine.

  **The C-6 hole is left open on purpose and pinned by a test.** The journal is local, so a truncator who also deletes it leaves no trace. The tamper-resistant corroborator is refs/heads/warpline-stakes, but reading it needs an async verifyFabric while backup.ts calls it synchronously. Documented as future work with a test that keeps the gap visible rather than letting it look closed.

  ## The builder corrected my brief and was right

  I twice specified that shadow refusals should audit ok:false. The builder refused to implement it and argued: daemonAudit.ok means "the dispatch produced a result", mcp-skin.test.ts:242 pins exactly that, PW-8's answer to masking was the additive resultCode field, and flipping ok on the shadow path alone would contradict the same brief's "non-shadow unchanged" clause. All verifiable, all correct. The defect was that the refusal was INVISIBLE; making it visible was the fix.

  Worth recording because the instruction to push back has to actually be honoured to be real, and this is the second time in two days a builder has done it (the first being the C-4 / stake recover collision).

  ## Re-scored grid

  Durability ABSENT to HOLDS. Recoverability PARTIAL to HOLDS. Immutable history PARTIAL to HOLDS. Repair path ABSENT to HOLDS. Bounded blast radius ABSENT/INVERTED to PARTIAL (--root exists; replication does not). No-silent-overwrite PARTIAL to HOLDS. Fail-closed-on-unknown PARTIAL to HOLDS on the write path.

  Unchanged: concurrency safety, still PARTIAL and still untested in anger.

  ## What I would still refuse to sign

  The concurrency surface has never run: zero KNOTs, zero attributed strands, every seal serial and anonymous. Migrating to refs mode engaged the CAS, but the R2 gate STILL cannot produce a KNOT because Jinx's C-9 second structural break is unfixed — admit short-circuits to FAST_ADMIT. Every concurrency verdict remains "holds because nothing has stressed it."

  C-13 is recoverable, not prevented — fsync makes an append durable, not atomic.

  Replication is absent. One fabric, one disk, one manual backup verb. Git's blast radius is bounded by every clone being a full copy; Warpline has no analogue and that is a design decision not yet consciously made.

  A swarm still wedges on its first genuine conflict: no abandon verb.</body>
