  <learnings>["The witness for a missing integrity check is often already on disk. C-6's anti-truncation evidence — a pickId per seal in .warpline/stakes/audit.jsonl — had been written for months and verify.ts had never imported it. Before building a new mechanism, check what the system already records and ignores.", "A check that fires on legitimate configurations is worse than the hole it closes, because it teaches people to ignore the tool. C-6 is advisory-in/hard-out: no journal, disabled valve, or fresh fabric all yield an empty attestation set and cannot fail.", "Argue severity from symmetry with an existing rule rather than from judgement. `stake-journal-orphan` is HARD because `ref-unresolved` is already HARD for the same class of evidence and a stake has stronger provenance — that reasoning survives a reviewer who disagrees about the feature.", "A literal reading of a remediation brief can be the data-loss event. 'Truncate to the last well-formed strand' is right for a tail tear and catastrophic mid-ledger. Builders should specialize and say so; briefs are written by someone who has not read the code as closely as the implementer will.", "Leaving a hole open, documenting it, and PINNING IT WITH A TEST is better than closing it badly. The C-6 journal is local and deletable; the async git-side corroborator conflicts with a synchronous verifyFabric. A test that keeps the gap visible prevents it reading as closed.", "The instruction to push back only counts if it is honoured. Two briefs of mine were wrong in two days — the C-4/stake-recover collision and the ok:false audit semantics — and both were caught by a builder refusing rather than complying. Record the correction in the commit, not just the fix."]</learnings>
